    sources: [{ id: "src_dario_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Rubén Darío (Q173767), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-06): description 'Nicaraguan poet, journalist and diplomat (1867-1916)', born 18 January 1867 (a second entry lists 1869), died 6 February 1916, citizenship Nicaragua, Korean label 루벤 다리오 (Korean Wikipedia article 루벤 다리오). Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q173767" }, { id: "src_dario_ebsco", kind: "biography", title: "William Gahan, 'Rubén Darío', EBSCO Research Starters (2024; read). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: calls him the most influential Spanish American writer of modernismo, says Azul (1888) broke with ornate Romantic style, Prosas profanas (1896) experimented with rhythm, meter and sound, and Cantos de vida y esperanza (1905) gathered over a decade of work; that Juan Valera's prologues to later editions of Azul brought him fame in the Americas and Europe; that journalism was his steady income across Central America, Buenos Aires and Europe and he wrote for La Nación; that he revised earlier works repeatedly and mixed prose and poetry; and that Lorca, Neruda, Borges and Paz regarded him as foundational although the avant-garde later made modernism seem dated. Its notes on his private life (drinking, relationships, health, death) are excluded from scoring.", url: "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/ruben-dario/" }, { id: "src_dario_gale", kind: "press", title: "'Rubén Darío', Encyclopedia of World Biography (Gale, via Encyclopedia.com; no named author; read). Gives the sequence Primeras notas (1885), Azul (1888, written in Chile, 'considered the first book of Spanish American modernism'), Los raros (1896, essays on writers he admired), Prosas profanas (1896), Cantos de vida y esperanza (his most mature collection) and later works judged weaker; says he founded newspapers in El Salvador (1889) and Guatemala (1890), wrote for La Epoca in Chile, was tied to La Nacion of Buenos Aires from 1889 and was sent by it to Spain in 1898; the page's dates for Azul and Cantos differ from the Oxford World Encyclopedia entry it also carries.", url: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/literature-and-arts/latin-american-literature-biographies/ruben-dario" }, { id: "src_dario_poets", kind: "institution", title: "Academy of American Poets, 'Rubén Darío' (poets.org; read). Short biography: left Nicaragua in his late teens for El Salvador and Chile, Colombian consul in Buenos Aires from 1893, left for Europe in 1898 and wrote as a correspondent for La Nación; Azul (1888) 'is often considered' the beginning of Spanish American modernism, Prosas profanas (1896), Cantos de vida y esperanza (1905); quotes Octavio Paz calling him one of the great modern poets. Brief and admiring.", url: "https://poets.org/poet/ruben-dario" }, { id: "src_dario_scarano", kind: "biography", title: "Laura Scarano, 'Una cristalina muralla de hielo: la resistencia antidariana en Espana' (scholarly article, 2016, Mar del Plata; the journal is not identified in the PDF; read in full). COUNTER-VOICE: studies the resistance Darío met in Spain: Clarin's 1893 dismissal of him as a derivative versifier, Unamuno's reservations about French influence, Cernuda's later hostility, and a racialised mockery as 'indio'; also records his Spanish friendships, his 1892 and 1898 visits (the second as La Nacion's correspondent), and his reply 'Pro domo mea' published in La Nacion. The article concentrates on reception, not on his working habits.", url: "https://dialnet.unirioja.es/descarga/articulo/5665267.pdf" }, { id: "src_dario_losraros", kind: "archive", title: "Ruben Dario, Los raros (second edition, Barcelona: Maucci, 1905; scan from the Academia Argentina de Letras collection shared through Wikimedia Argentina, read for its frontispiece page). Portrait source record for the image used: the printed portrait is captioned 'Ruben Dario' and signed by its artist. Not used as support for any row.", url: "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Los_raros_-_Ruben_Dario_-_2aed_-_1905.pdf" }],
